### What is the Permutations Problem?

Permutations asks you to generate every possible ordering of a set of distinct elements. For n elements, there are n! (n factorial) permutations. This is one of the most fundamental backtracking problems.

At National Bank of India, Rajesh needs to stress-test the settlement engine by replaying a batch of payments in every possible order.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class PermutationGenerator {
    public static List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        boolean[] used = new boolean[nums.length];
        backtrack(nums, used, new ArrayList<>(), result);
        return result;
    }

    private static void backtrack(int[] nums, boolean[] used,
            List<Integer> current, List<List<Integer>> result) {
        if (current.size() == nums.length) {
            result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));  // defensive copy
            return;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (used[i]) continue;
            used[i] = true;
            current.add(nums[i]);
            backtrack(nums, used, current, result);
            current.remove(current.size() - 1);  // unchoose
            used[i] = false;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] paymentIds = {1001, 1002, 1003};
        List<List<Integer>> orders = permute(paymentIds);
        System.out.println("Total orderings: " + orders.size());
        for (List<Integer> order : orders) {
            System.out.println(order);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Total orderings: 6
[1001, 1002, 1003]
[1001, 1003, 1002]
[1002, 1001, 1003]
[1002, 1003, 1001]
[1003, 1001, 1002]
[1003, 1002, 1001]
```

### Why Does This Problem Matter?

Permutation generation is the basis for brute-force solutions to ordering problems: scheduling, arrangement, and optimization. Many interview problems reduce to finding the best permutation or checking all permutations.

In banking, the order in which transactions are processed can affect overdraft fees, hold releases, and final balances. Testing every order ensures the system produces consistent results regardless of sequence.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class OrderSensitiveSettlement {
    public static BigDecimal settle(int[] paymentOrder, BigDecimal startBalance) {
        BigDecimal balance = startBalance;
        for (int payment : paymentOrder) {
            balance = balance.subtract(new BigDecimal(payment));
            if (balance.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) < 0) {
                balance = balance.subtract(new BigDecimal("35"));  // overdraft fee
            }
        }
        return balance;
    }
}
```

### How Does the Backtracking Technique Work?

The backtracking follows the choose-explore-unchoose pattern. At each recursion level, we loop over all elements, skip used ones, choose one by marking it used and adding it to the current list, recurse to fill the next position, then unchoose by removing it and clearing the used flag.

The discipline is that after every branch, the state is restored exactly so sibling branches start clean.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class BacktrackingExplained {
    public static void backtrack(String[] items, boolean[] used,
            List<String> current, int depth) {
        String indent = "  ".repeat(depth);
        if (current.size() == items.length) {
            System.out.println(indent + "COMPLETE: " + current);
            return;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
            if (used[i]) continue;
            System.out.println(indent + "CHOOSE: " + items[i]);
            used[i] = true;
            current.add(items[i]);
            backtrack(items, used, current, depth + 1);
            current.remove(current.size() - 1);
            used[i] = false;
            System.out.println(indent + "UNCHOOSE: " + items[i]);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] payments = {"P1", "P2"};
        backtrack(payments, new boolean[2], new ArrayList<>(), 0);
    }
}
```

```text
CHOOSE: P1
  CHOOSE: P2
    COMPLETE: [P1, P2]
  UNCHOOSE: P2
UNCHOOSE: P1
CHOOSE: P2
  CHOOSE: P1
    COMPLETE: [P2, P1]
  UNCHOOSE: P1
UNCHOOSE: P2
```

### How Is This Different from Subsets?

| Aspect | Permutations | Subsets |
|---|---|---|
| Output count | n! orderings | 2^n subsets |
| Order matters | Yes, [1,2] differs from [2,1] | No, {1,2} = {2,1} |
| Element usage | Every element appears exactly once | Each element is included or excluded |
| Technique | Used-array backtracking | Iterative doubling or start-index backtracking |
| Growth rate | Factorial (much faster) | Exponential |

```java
// Subsets: 2^3 = 8 results, order does not matter
// Permutations: 3! = 6 results, order matters
```

### What Are the Rules and Constraints?

The input must contain distinct elements for the basic version. Each element appears exactly once in every permutation. The used array ensures no element is picked twice. The total output is n!, so this is only feasible for small n. For n = 10 there are 3,628,800 permutations, and for n = 12 there are about 479 million.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PermutationFeasibility {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long fact = 1;
        for (int n = 1; n <= 12; n++) {
            fact *= n;
            System.out.println(n + "! = " + fact);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
