2! = 2
3! = 6
4! = 24
5! = 120
6! = 720
7! = 5040
8! = 40320
9! = 362880
10! = 3628800
11! = 39916800
12! = 479001600
```

### What About Duplicate Elements?

When the input contains duplicates, the basic approach produces duplicate permutations. The fix is to sort the input first and skip elements that are equal to their predecessor if the predecessor was not used at the same level.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class PermutationsWithDuplicates {
    public static List<List<Integer>> permuteUnique(int[] nums) {
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        backtrack(nums, new boolean[nums.length], new ArrayList<>(), result);
        return result;
    }

    private static void backtrack(int[] nums, boolean[] used,
            List<Integer> current, List<List<Integer>> result) {
        if (current.size() == nums.length) {
            result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
            return;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (used[i]) continue;
            // Skip duplicate at same level
            if (i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i - 1] && !used[i - 1]) continue;
            used[i] = true;
            current.add(nums[i]);
            backtrack(nums, used, current, result);
            current.remove(current.size() - 1);
            used[i] = false;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] amounts = {100, 100, 200};
        System.out.println(permuteUnique(amounts));
    }
}
```

```text
[[100, 100, 200], [100, 200, 100], [200, 100, 100]]
```

### What Are Common Mistakes?

The first common mistake is forgetting the defensive copy. If you add the current list directly to the result without copying, all entries end up pointing to the same empty list after backtracking.

Wrong code:

```java
// WRONG: all entries in result will be empty after backtracking
result.add(current);  // adds reference, not a copy
```

Correct code:

```java
// CORRECT: store a snapshot of the current state
result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));  // defensive copy
```

The second mistake is forgetting to unchoose. Without restoring the used flag, the element stays permanently marked and later branches cannot use it.

### What About the Swap-Based Approach?

An alternative to the used-array approach is the swap-based method. At each position i, swap element i with each element from i to the end, recurse for position i+1, then swap back.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class PermutationsSwap {
    public static List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        backtrack(nums, 0, result);
        return result;
    }

    private static void backtrack(int[] nums, int start, List<List<Integer>> result) {
        if (start == nums.length) {
            List<Integer> perm = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int n : nums) perm.add(n);
            result.add(perm);
            return;
        }
        for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
            swap(nums, start, i);
            backtrack(nums, start + 1, result);
            swap(nums, start, i);  // swap back
        }
    }

    private static void swap(int[] arr, int a, int b) {
        int temp = arr[a]; arr[a] = arr[b]; arr[b] = temp;
    }
}
```

### What Are Common Interview Follow-Up Questions?

Interviewers commonly ask these follow-ups:

- Handle duplicates (Permutations II): sort and skip equal siblings as shown above.
- Find the next permutation in lexicographic order: this uses a specific scan-and-swap algorithm.
- Find the k-th permutation directly without generating all of them: use factorial number system.
- Count permutations of a multiset: use the formula n! / (count1! * count2! * ...).

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PermutationCount {
    // Count permutations of a multiset
    public static long countDistinctPermutations(int[] freq) {
        int total = 0;
        for (int f : freq) total += f;
        long numerator = factorial(total);
        long denominator = 1;
        for (int f : freq) denominator *= factorial(f);
        return numerator / denominator;
    }

    private static long factorial(int n) {
        long result = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) result *= i;
        return result;
    }
}
```
